 pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Printed on it: Parent DNA molecules | Replication fork | Replication | bubbles |","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"Each bubble has two replication forks that move in opposite directions from the origin, as the outward arrows show. This unwinds more parental DNA and copies it, so the bubble enlarges. New origins are not added in the centre. Replication is bidirectional, not one-directional. Parental strands stay separated at the forks and do not rejoin.","subtopic_id":454,"topic_label":"Importance of DNA replication","correct_answer":1},{"id":4,"type":"mcq","source":{"area":"Importance of DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"options":["By ensuring the consistent inheritance of traits from one generation to the next","By enabling rapid adaptation to environmental changes","By increasing the number of genes in each generation","By allowing genetic variation through mutation"],"question":"In what way does the accurate replication of DNA contribute to the survival of species?","topic_id":98,"explanation":"The correct answer is 'By ensuring the consistent inheritance of traits from one generation to the next'. Accurate DNA replication ensures that the genetic blueprint is faithfully passed on, which is essential for the survival and stability of species. By allowing genetic variation through mutation is incorrect because while mutations can lead to genetic variation, accurate replication minimises errors that could lead to harmful mutations. By enabling rapid adaptation to environmental changes is incorrect because rapid adaptation usually involves mutations and natural selection, not just DNA replication. By increasing the number of genes in each generation is incorrect because DNA replication does not increase the number of genes; it duplicates the existing genes.","