_id":"7fcab68f-1c7d-4f81-934e-6ef3faea9a7e","answers":{"mcq":{},"longform":{},"interactive":{}},"score":0,"total_questions":10,"percentage":"0.00","grade":"D","feedback":{"text":"Great effort! Review the criteria and explanations below to improve."},"questions_data":[{"id":1,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Identify the role of Part (C) in the DNA molecule — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Correct identification of; Explanation of their role in DNA stability."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Structure of nucleic acids—DNA and RNA","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Identify the role of Part (C) in the DNA molecule.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing the structure of a DNA molecule with labelled parts (A), (B), (C), and (D). Part (A) represents the sugar-phosphate backbone, Part (B) represents the nucleotide bases, Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds, and Part (D) represents the double helix structure.","topic_id":97,"explanation":"Explain the function of hydrogen bonds in DNA structure.","subtopic_id":448,"topic_label":"Structure of nucleic acids—DNA and RNA","model_answer":"Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds between the nucleotide bases. These hydrogen bonds play a crucial role in stabilizing the DNA double helix by holding the two strands together. Without these bonds, the DNA strands would not remain paired and the structure would be unstable.","exam_question_id":"8aa9c203-58fc-45aa-9f3c-1a0f8bc8d7fc:2","marking_criteria":["Correct identification of hydrogen bonds","Explanation of their role in DNA stability"]},{"id":2,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the directionality of DNA synthesis."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Significance of telomere in permitting continued replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the antiparallel orientation of the two DNA strands is important for DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing the structure of a DNA molecule with labelled parts (A), (B), (C), and (D). Part (A) represents the sugar-phosphate backbone, Part (B) represents the nucleotide bases, Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds, and Part (D) represents the double helix structure.","topic_id":97,"explanation":"Describe the significance of antiparallel strands in DNA replication.","subtopic_id":450,"topic_label":"Significance of telomere in permitting continued replication","model_answer":"The antiparallel orientation of the two DNA strands means that they run in opposite directions (5' to 3' and 3' to 5'). This is important for DNA replication because it allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to add new nucleotides in the correct direction. DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand, so the antiparallel orientation ensures that both strands can be replicated simultaneously and accurately.","exam_question_id":"8aa9c203-58fc-45aa-9f3c-1a0f8bc8d7fc:2","marking_criteria":["Explanation of the antiparallel orientation","Explanation of its importance in DNA replication","Correct final answer"]},{"id":3,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you are asked to describe the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication. You need to explain how these bubbles form, their function in unwinding the DNA, and the role of replication forks within them. Your answer should be based on the information provided in the diagram.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"Replication bubbles form at the origins of replication where the DNA double helix is unwound. These bubbles allow the two strands of DNA to separate, providing access for the replication machinery to synthesize new strands. Replication forks are active sites within these bubbles where DNA polymerase enzymes synthesize new DNA strands by adding nucleotides complementary to the parental strands. The bubbles expand as replication proceeds, enabling the simultaneous replication of multiple segments of the DNA molecule.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Describe the formation of replication bubbles.","Explain how replication bubbles facilitate the unwinding of the DNA double helix.","Describe the function of replication forks within the bubbles."]},{"id":4,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the directionality of DNA synthesis and the role of complementary base pairing."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Experimental evidence of semi-conservative DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe how the synthesis of daughter strands occurs at the replication fork and explain why this synthesis is semi-conservative.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you need to describe the process of DNA synthesis at the replication fork. You should explain how the daughter strands are synthesised and why the replication process is termed semi-conservative. Your answer should be based on the information provided in the diagram and your understanding of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":455,"topic_label":"Experimental evidence of semi-conservative DNA replication","model_answer":"At the replication fork, DNA polymerase enzymes synthesize new daughter strands by adding nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand. Each new daughter strand is synthesized in the 5' to 3' direction, complementary to the parental strand. This means that one parental strand serves as a template for the synthesis of a new daughter strand. Because each new DNA molecule contains one old (parental) strand and one new (daughter) strand, the process is described as semi-conservative.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Explain the process of DNA synthesis at the replication fork.","Describe how each new daughter strand is synthesised.","Explain why the replication process is described as semi-conservative."]},{"id":5,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: If an error occurs during DNA replication, such as a mismatch in base pairing, describe a possible consequence and explain how cells have mechanisms to correct such errors — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Discuss the importance of error…."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Importance of DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"If an error occurs during DNA replication, such as a mismatch in base pairing, describe a possible consequence and explain how cells have mechanisms to correct such errors.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you need to describe a possible consequence of a replication error and explain how cells correct such errors. You should also discuss the importance of these error-correction mechanisms in maintaining genetic integrity. Your answer should be based on your understanding of DNA replication and repair processes.","subtopic_id":454,"topic_label":"Importance of DNA replication","model_answer":"A replication error, such as a mismatch in base pairing, can lead to mutations. Mutations can result in changes to the genetic information, potentially affecting protein function and cellular processes. Cells have several mechanisms to correct replication errors, including proofreading by DNA polymerases, which can remove incorrectly paired nucleotides, and mismatch repair systems that recognize and repair errors after replication. These mechanisms are crucial for maintaining the integrity of the genetic code and preventing the accumulation of harmful mutations.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Describe a possible consequence of a replication error.","Explain how cells correct replication errors.","Discuss the importance of error correction in maintaining genetic integrity."]},{"id":6,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Think about the function of enzymes in biological processes."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Identify the enzyme shown in part (a) and describe its role during DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"DNA helicase is an enzyme that plays a critical role in DNA replication by unwinding and separating the two strands of the DNA double helix. This creates a replication fork, which allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to access the separated strands and synthesise new complementary strands. DNA helicase is essential for maintaining the stability of the replication fork and ensuring accurate DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The enzyme shown in part (a) is DNA helicase. Its role during DNA replication is to unwind and separate the two strands of the DNA double helix, creating a replication fork. This allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to access the separated strands and synthesize new complementary strands. DNA helicase is crucial for maintaining the stability of the replication fork and ensuring accurate DNA replication.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies the enzyme in part (a)","Describes the role of the enzyme in DNA replication","Explains the importance of the enzyme in maintaining the stability of the replication fork"]},{"id":7,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Answer every labelled part of the question, and link your points so a marker can follow the full argument."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe the process of DNA replication as shown in the diagram. Include the role of the enzyme in part (a) and the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"DNA replication is a complex process that involves the unwinding of the DNA double helix by DNA helicase, creating a replication fork. At the replication forks, DNA polymerase synthesises new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands. This results in the formation of two identical DNA molecules, ensuring the accurate replication of genetic information.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"DNA replication begins with the unwinding of the DNA double helix by the enzyme shown in part (a), which is DNA helicase. This enzyme separates the two strands of the DNA molecule, creating a replication fork. At the replication forks (parts (b) and (d)), DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands. The newly synthesized short DNA segments (part (c)) are added to the growing DNA strand, resulting in the formation of two identical DNA molecules. This process ensures the accurate replication of genetic information.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Describes the process of DNA replication accurately","Explains the role of the enzyme in part (a) during DNA replication","Describes the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks"]},{"id":8,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: the of a failure in this."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"The synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication because it ensures the accurate duplication of genetic information. Without this synthesis, the replication process would fail, leading to errors in the genetic information and potentially harmful consequences for the organism.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication because it ensures the accurate duplication of genetic information. DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands at the replication forks. This process guarantees that each new DNA molecule contains an identical copy of the original genetic material. Without this synthesis, the replication process would fail, leading to errors in the genetic information and potentially harmful consequences for the organism.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Explains the importance of the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks","Describes the consequences of a failure in this process"]},{"id":9,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the total number of chromosomes shown in the karyotype — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Reference to the diagram."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the total number of chromosomes shown in the karyotype.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Count each pair as two chromosomes and add the unpaired ones to find the total number of chromosomes in the karyotype.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"The total number of chromosomes is 5 pairs × 2 + 7 pairs × 2 + 6 pairs × 2 + 4 pairs × 2 + 2 unpaired = 10 + 14 + 12 + 8 + 2 = 46 chromosomes.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Correct total number of chromosomes","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":10,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the significance of the two unpaired chromosomes in the inset — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Explanation of their significance; Reference to the diagram."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the significance of the two unpaired chromosomes in the inset.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Unpaired chromosomes can result in genetic disorders due to an abnormal number of chromosomes, which can affect normal development and function.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"The two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small, may indicate a genetic disorder or condition such as aneuploidy, where there is an abnormal number of chromosomes. The presence of unpaired chromosomes can lead to genetic diseases or developmental issues.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Identification of unpaired chromosomes","Explanation of their significance","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":11,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the role of specific genes in normal development and function."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe how a mutation in one of the chromosomes might affect an individual.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Mutations can lead to a range of effects depending on the gene involved and the type of mutation, potentially causing genetic disorders or having no noticeable impact.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"A mutation in one of the chromosomes, such as a deletion, duplication, or translocation, can lead to a variety of effects depending on the gene affected. For example, a mutation in a gene responsible for a specific protein might result in a genetic disorder, while a mutation in a non-coding region might have no noticeable effect. The impact of the mutation can range from mild to severe, affecting various aspects of an individual's health and development.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Explanation of mutation effects","Reference to specific chromosomes or genes","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":12,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Use the genetic code table to find the amino acids."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Name the amino acids that are coded by the mRNA sequence provided.","scenario":"You are given a sample of mRNA with the sequence AUG-UAC-CGA-UGA. Using this sequence, identify the corresponding amino acids produced during translation.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Using the genetic code table, identify the start codon AUG as Methionine (Met). Translate each codon to its respective amino acid. UGA is a stop codon and terminates translation.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"Start codon AUG codes for Methionine (Met). UAC codes for Tyrosine (Tyr). CGA codes for Arginine (Arg). UGA is a stop codon and does not code for an amino acid.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:1","marking_criteria":["Identify the start codon (AUG)","Identify and name the three codons","Match each codon to its corresponding amino acid"]},{"id":13,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the role of the ribosome during the translation process — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Discuss the role of in this process."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the role of the ribosome during the translation process.","scenario":"You are given a sample of mRNA with the sequence AUG-UAC-CGA-UGA. Using this sequence, identify the corresponding amino acids produced during translation.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"The ribosome is the site of protein synthesis. It binds to mRNA and ensures the correct sequence of amino acids is produced by reading the mRNA sequence in codons and facilitating the addition of amino acids by tRNA.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The ribosome facilitates protein synthesis by binding to mRNA and facilitating the decoding of the mRNA sequence into a polypeptide chain. It reads the mRNA sequence in codons and ensures the correct amino acids, brought by tRNA molecules, are added to the growing polypeptide chain.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:1","marking_criteria":["Describe the ribosome's role in protein synthesis","Explain how the ribosome reads the mRNA sequence","Discuss the role of tRNA in this process"]},{"id":14,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the temperature at which the rate of protein synthesis is the highest — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: the corresponding temperature."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the temperature at which the rate of protein synthesis is the highest.","scenario":"A scientist is investigating the effect of temperature on the rate of protein synthesis. The following data is collected at different temperatures: \nTemperature (°C): 20, 30, 40, 50 \nRate of Protein Synthesis (proteins/min): 40, 60, 80, 50","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Compare the rates of protein synthesis at each temperature and identify the highest value.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The highest rate of protein synthesis is 80 proteins/min at 40°C.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:3","marking_criteria":["Identify the highest rate of protein synthesis","State the corresponding temperature"]},{"id":15,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the effect of temperature on enzyme structure and function."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of protein synthesis decreases at 50°C despite being higher at 40°C.","scenario":"A scientist is investigating the effect of temperature on the rate of protein synthesis. The following data is collected at different temperatures: \nTemperature (°C): 20, 30, 40, 50 \nRate of Protein Synthesis (proteins/min): 40, 60, 80, 50","topic_id":100,"explanation":"High temperatures can denature enzymes, making them inactive. This reduces the efficiency of the protein synthesis process, resulting in a lower rate of protein synthesis at 50°C.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The rate of protein synthesis decreases at 50°C because high temperatures can denature enzymes, rendering them inactive. This inactivation reduces the efficiency of the translation process, leading to a lower rate of protein synthesis.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:3","marking_criteria":["Identify the impact of high temperature on enzyme activity","Explain the denaturation of enzymes at high temperatures","Discuss the effect on the rate of protein synthesis"]},{"id":16,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment.","scenario":"During a practical session, students are investigating the effect of different concentrations of a transcription inhibitor on the rate of transcription in yeast cells. They set up the experiment with varying concentrations of the inhibitor and measure the amount of RNA produced over a fixed time period.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"The independent variable is the one you change (concentration of inhibitor), and the dependent variable is what you measure (amount of RNA produced).","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"Independent Variable: Concentration of transcription inhibitor\nDependent Variable: Amount of RNA produced","exam_question_id":"d305d746-7b29-4d07-96a0-5bee2ed76671:2","marking_criteria":["Correctly identify the independent variable.","Correctly identify the dependent variable."]},{"id":17,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Relate the observed to the expected biological outcome."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases.","scenario":"During a practical session, students are investigating the effect of different concentrations of a transcription inhibitor on the rate of transcription in yeast cells. They set up the experiment with varying concentrations of the inhibitor and measure the amount of RNA produced over a fixed time period.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Increasing the concentration of the inhibitor reduces the activity of RNA polymerase, which in turn decreases the amount of RNA produced.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The transcription inhibitor binds to the RNA polymerase enzyme, reducing its ability to catalyze the transcription process. As the concentration of the inhibitor increases, more enzyme molecules are inhibited, leading to a decrease in the overall rate of transcription. This results in a lower amount of RNA being produced.","exam_question_id":"d305d746-7b29-4d07-96a0-5bee2ed76671:2","marking_criteria":["Explain the role of the transcription inhibitor.","Describe the effect of increasing inhibitor concentration on the transcription process.","Relate the observed results to the expected biological outcome."]},{"id":18,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Look at the table to find the rate of DNA replication that corresponds to the given concentration of DNA polymerase."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Calculate the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 3 units/mL.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication in a laboratory experiment.\n\n| Concentration of DNA Polymerase (units/mL) | Rate of DNA Replication (base pairs/min) |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| 1                                        | 50                                     |\n| 2                                        | 100                                    |\n| 3                                        | 150                                    |\n| 4                                        | 200                                    |\n| 5                                        | 250                                    |","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Using the table, the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 3 units/mL is 150 base pairs/min.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"150 base pairs/min","exam_question_id":"37f21a4c-8b98-4bf9-af7a-cb7502b97f0d:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies the concentration of DNA polymerase from the table.","Correctly identifies the corresponding rate of DNA replication from the table."]},{"id":19,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Correctly identifies that DNA polymerase is an involved in DNA replication.; that a rate of DNA replication is…."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication in a laboratory experiment.\n\n| Concentration of DNA Polymerase (units/mL) | Rate of DNA Replication (base pairs/min) |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| 1                                        | 50                                     |\n| 2                                        | 100                                    |\n| 3                                        | 150                                    |\n| 4                                        | 200                                    |\n| 5                                        | 250                                    |","topic_id":98,"explanation":"The rate of DNA replication is directly proportional to the concentration of DNA polymerase. DNA polymerase is an enzyme that catalyses the synthesis of DNA. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, more active sites become available for DNA synthesis. This allows for a higher rate of DNA replication because more DNA strands can be synthesised simultaneously. Therefore, an increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase leads to an increase in the rate of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases because DNA polymerase is an enzyme that catalyzes the synthesis of DNA. An increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase provides more active sites for DNA synthesis, allowing for a higher rate of DNA replication. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, more DNA strands can be synthesized simultaneously, leading to a faster overall rate of DNA replication.","exam_question_id":"37f21a4c-8b98-4bf9-af7a-cb7502b97f0d:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies that DNA polymerase is an enzyme involved in DNA replication.","Explains that an increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase leads to more active sites available for DNA synthesis.","Explains that a higher rate of DNA replication is achieved due to the increased number of active sites and faster synthesis of DNA strands."]},{"id":20,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Refer to the data table provided in the scenario."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Calculate the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 2 units/mL.","scenario":"Consider a laboratory experiment where the concentration of DNA polymerase is varied to observe its effect on the rate of DNA replication. The following data table shows the relationship between the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication.","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Use the data table to find the rate of DNA replication for the given concentration of DNA polymerase.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The concentration of DNA polymerase is 2 units/mL, which corresponds to a rate of DNA replication of 100 base pairs/min.","exam_question_id":"7a7822e7-cc5a-4c91-a991-d34c059e0bcd:1","marking_criteria":["Identify the correct concentration of DNA polymerase in the table.","Identify the corresponding rate of DNA replication from the table."]},{"id":21,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Provide a logical explanation for the observed trend in the."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","scenario":"Consider a laboratory experiment where the concentration of DNA polymerase is varied to observe its effect on the rate of DNA replication. The following data table shows the relationship between the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication.","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Explain the role of DNA polymerase in DNA replication and how an increase in its concentration can affect the rate of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"DNA polymerase is an enzyme that synthesizes new DNA strands during replication. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, there are more enzyme molecules available to catalyze the replication process, leading to a higher rate of DNA replication. This is supported by the data table, which shows an increase in the rate of DNA replication as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","exam_question_id":"7a7822e7-cc5a-4c91-a991-d34c059e0bcd:1","marking_criteria":["Explain the role of DNA polymerase in DNA replication.","Explain how an increase in DNA polymerase concentration affects the rate of DNA replication.","Provide a logical explanation for the observed trend in the data table."]},{"id":22,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Nature of genes (Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids)","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of a transcription inhibitor and the rate of transcription in yeast cells. The table records the concentration of the inhibitor (in micromolar, µM) and the corresponding rate of transcription (in RNA molecules produced per minute).","topic_id":97,"explanation":"The independent variable is what you change in the experiment, and the dependent variable is what you measure. In this experiment, the concentration of the transcription inhibitor is changed, and the rate of transcription is measured.","topic_label":"Nature of genes (Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids)","model_answer":"The independent variable is the concentration of the transcription inhibitor, and the dependent variable is the rate of transcription.","exam_question_id":"132fb80d-27c7-4874-a87d-ef946ffd0d9d:1","marking_criteria":["Identify the variable that is changed by the experimenter (independent variable).","Identify the variable that is measured or observed (dependent variable)."]},{"id":23,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: the role of the inhibitor in the process.; how increasing the concentration of the inhibitor affects the process."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Enzymes and proteins involved in DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of a transcription inhibitor and the rate of transcription in yeast cells. The table records the concentration of the inhibitor (in micromolar, µM) and the corresponding rate of transcription (in RNA molecules produced per minute).","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Transcription inhibitors bind to and block the activity of enzymes involved in transcription, such as RNA polymerase. As the concentration of the inhibitor increases, more enzymes are blocked, leading to a decrease in the rate of transcription. This results in a lower amount of RNA being produced over a fixed time period.","subtopic_id":456,"topic_label":"Enzymes and proteins involved in DNA replication","model_answer":"The transcription inhibitor binds to and blocks the activity of enzymes involved in transcription, such as RNA polymerase. As the concentration of the inhibitor increases, more enzymes are blocked, leading to a decrease in the rate of transcription. This results in a lower amount of RNA being produced over a fixed time period.","exam_question_id":"132fb80d-27c7-4874-a87d-ef946ffd0d9d:1","marking_criteria":["Explain the role of the transcription inhibitor in the transcription process.","Describe how increasing the concentration of the inhibitor affects the transcription process.","Explain the relationship between the concentration of the inhibitor and the amount of RNA produced."]}],"longform_grades":{},"time_taken":3659,"submission_reason":"manual","violation_count":0,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:23:51.107Z","assessment_subject":"Biology S5 SB ","assessment_difficulty":"Exam Style (Past Papers)","assessments":{"subject":"Biology S5 SB ","difficulty":"Exam Style (Past Papers)","topics":[]}},{"id":"e93896c4-91b2-4193-85d0-5d9453ba7084","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","assessment_id":"7fcab68f-1c7d-4f81-934e-6ef3faea9a7e","answers":{"mcq":{},"longform":{},"interactive":{}},"score":0,"total_questions":10,"percentage":"0.00","grade":"D","feedback":{"text":"Great effort! Review the criteria and explanations below to improve."},"questions_data":[{"id":1,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Identify the role of Part (C) in the DNA molecule — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Correct identification of; Explanation of their role in DNA stability."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Structure of nucleic acids—DNA and RNA","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Identify the role of Part (C) in the DNA molecule.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing the structure of a DNA molecule with labelled parts (A), (B), (C), and (D). Part (A) represents the sugar-phosphate backbone, Part (B) represents the nucleotide bases, Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds, and Part (D) represents the double helix structure.","topic_id":97,"explanation":"Explain the function of hydrogen bonds in DNA structure.","subtopic_id":448,"topic_label":"Structure of nucleic acids—DNA and RNA","model_answer":"Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds between the nucleotide bases. These hydrogen bonds play a crucial role in stabilizing the DNA double helix by holding the two strands together. Without these bonds, the DNA strands would not remain paired and the structure would be unstable.","exam_question_id":"8aa9c203-58fc-45aa-9f3c-1a0f8bc8d7fc:2","marking_criteria":["Correct identification of hydrogen bonds","Explanation of their role in DNA stability"]},{"id":2,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the directionality of DNA synthesis."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Significance of telomere in permitting continued replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the antiparallel orientation of the two DNA strands is important for DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing the structure of a DNA molecule with labelled parts (A), (B), (C), and (D). Part (A) represents the sugar-phosphate backbone, Part (B) represents the nucleotide bases, Part (C) represents the hydrogen bonds, and Part (D) represents the double helix structure.","topic_id":97,"explanation":"Describe the significance of antiparallel strands in DNA replication.","subtopic_id":450,"topic_label":"Significance of telomere in permitting continued replication","model_answer":"The antiparallel orientation of the two DNA strands means that they run in opposite directions (5' to 3' and 3' to 5'). This is important for DNA replication because it allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to add new nucleotides in the correct direction. DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand, so the antiparallel orientation ensures that both strands can be replicated simultaneously and accurately.","exam_question_id":"8aa9c203-58fc-45aa-9f3c-1a0f8bc8d7fc:2","marking_criteria":["Explanation of the antiparallel orientation","Explanation of its importance in DNA replication","Correct final answer"]},{"id":3,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you are asked to describe the role of replication bubbles in the process of DNA replication. You need to explain how these bubbles form, their function in unwinding the DNA, and the role of replication forks within them. Your answer should be based on the information provided in the diagram.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"Replication bubbles form at the origins of replication where the DNA double helix is unwound. These bubbles allow the two strands of DNA to separate, providing access for the replication machinery to synthesize new strands. Replication forks are active sites within these bubbles where DNA polymerase enzymes synthesize new DNA strands by adding nucleotides complementary to the parental strands. The bubbles expand as replication proceeds, enabling the simultaneous replication of multiple segments of the DNA molecule.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Describe the formation of replication bubbles.","Explain how replication bubbles facilitate the unwinding of the DNA double helix.","Describe the function of replication forks within the bubbles."]},{"id":4,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the directionality of DNA synthesis and the role of complementary base pairing."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Experimental evidence of semi-conservative DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe how the synthesis of daughter strands occurs at the replication fork and explain why this synthesis is semi-conservative.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you need to describe the process of DNA synthesis at the replication fork. You should explain how the daughter strands are synthesised and why the replication process is termed semi-conservative. Your answer should be based on the information provided in the diagram and your understanding of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":455,"topic_label":"Experimental evidence of semi-conservative DNA replication","model_answer":"At the replication fork, DNA polymerase enzymes synthesize new daughter strands by adding nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand. Each new daughter strand is synthesized in the 5' to 3' direction, complementary to the parental strand. This means that one parental strand serves as a template for the synthesis of a new daughter strand. Because each new DNA molecule contains one old (parental) strand and one new (daughter) strand, the process is described as semi-conservative.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Explain the process of DNA synthesis at the replication fork.","Describe how each new daughter strand is synthesised.","Explain why the replication process is described as semi-conservative."]},{"id":5,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: If an error occurs during DNA replication, such as a mismatch in base pairing, describe a possible consequence and explain how cells have mechanisms to correct such errors — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Discuss the importance of error…."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Importance of DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"If an error occurs during DNA replication, such as a mismatch in base pairing, describe a possible consequence and explain how cells have mechanisms to correct such errors.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled. Labels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication bubbles, Parental strands, Daughter strands","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"Three stages of DNA replication in eukaryotes are shown from top to bottom. At the top, parent DNA is two parallel lines with three black dots marking origins of replication. In the middle, a replication bubble forms at each origin, with a replication fork at the bubble ends and arrows pointing outward. At the bottom, the bubbles have grown larger, with arrows pointing away from the origins, and neighbouring bubbles are about to merge. Parental strands and daughter strands (the lighter inner strands) are labelled.\nLabels: Parent DNA molecules, Replication fork, Replication, bubbles, Parental s","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p96-f0.png","explanation":"In this part, you need to describe a possible consequence of a replication error and explain how cells correct such errors. You should also discuss the importance of these error-correction mechanisms in maintaining genetic integrity. Your answer should be based on your understanding of DNA replication and repair processes.","subtopic_id":454,"topic_label":"Importance of DNA replication","model_answer":"A replication error, such as a mismatch in base pairing, can lead to mutations. Mutations can result in changes to the genetic information, potentially affecting protein function and cellular processes. Cells have several mechanisms to correct replication errors, including proofreading by DNA polymerases, which can remove incorrectly paired nucleotides, and mismatch repair systems that recognize and repair errors after replication. These mechanisms are crucial for maintaining the integrity of the genetic code and preventing the accumulation of harmful mutations.","exam_question_id":"216a3e44-5383-4914-8b41-43648aed44aa:1","marking_criteria":["Describe a possible consequence of a replication error.","Explain how cells correct replication errors.","Discuss the importance of error correction in maintaining genetic integrity."]},{"id":6,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Think about the function of enzymes in biological processes."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Identify the enzyme shown in part (a) and describe its role during DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"DNA helicase is an enzyme that plays a critical role in DNA replication by unwinding and separating the two strands of the DNA double helix. This creates a replication fork, which allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to access the separated strands and synthesise new complementary strands. DNA helicase is essential for maintaining the stability of the replication fork and ensuring accurate DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The enzyme shown in part (a) is DNA helicase. Its role during DNA replication is to unwind and separate the two strands of the DNA double helix, creating a replication fork. This allows the DNA polymerase enzyme to access the separated strands and synthesize new complementary strands. DNA helicase is crucial for maintaining the stability of the replication fork and ensuring accurate DNA replication.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies the enzyme in part (a)","Describes the role of the enzyme in DNA replication","Explains the importance of the enzyme in maintaining the stability of the replication fork"]},{"id":7,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Answer every labelled part of the question, and link your points so a marker can follow the full argument."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe the process of DNA replication as shown in the diagram. Include the role of the enzyme in part (a) and the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"DNA replication is a complex process that involves the unwinding of the DNA double helix by DNA helicase, creating a replication fork. At the replication forks, DNA polymerase synthesises new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands. This results in the formation of two identical DNA molecules, ensuring the accurate replication of genetic information.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"DNA replication begins with the unwinding of the DNA double helix by the enzyme shown in part (a), which is DNA helicase. This enzyme separates the two strands of the DNA molecule, creating a replication fork. At the replication forks (parts (b) and (d)), DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands. The newly synthesized short DNA segments (part (c)) are added to the growing DNA strand, resulting in the formation of two identical DNA molecules. This process ensures the accurate replication of genetic information.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Describes the process of DNA replication accurately","Explains the role of the enzyme in part (a) during DNA replication","Describes the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks"]},{"id":8,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: the of a failure in this."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram of DNA replication showing labelled parts (a) to (d). Part (a) represents the enzyme that holds the DNA helix, part (b) and part (d) represent the replication forks, and part (c) represents a newly synthesised short DNA segment.","topic_id":98,"image_alt":"A diagram of DNA replication. A double-stranded DNA helix on the left is held by a purple C-shaped enzyme labelled (a) at the point where the strands unzip. The fork opens to the right into two branches, each with small circles and large oval shapes on the strands. The upper branch is labelled (d) and the lower branch is labelled (b). Arrows on the new strands show the direction of synthesis. (c) points to a newly made short segment on the lower strand. A long arrow at the top points left.\nLabels: (d), (c), (a), (b)\nOriginal exercise: 6.\t  (i)   Identify the structure shown in figure.\n(ii)\tWri","image_url":"/textbook-images/textbook-bank/8/p106-f0.png","explanation":"The synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication because it ensures the accurate duplication of genetic information. Without this synthesis, the replication process would fail, leading to errors in the genetic information and potentially harmful consequences for the organism.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks is essential for DNA replication because it ensures the accurate duplication of genetic information. DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary DNA strands by adding nucleotides to the exposed template strands at the replication forks. This process guarantees that each new DNA molecule contains an identical copy of the original genetic material. Without this synthesis, the replication process would fail, leading to errors in the genetic information and potentially harmful consequences for the organism.","exam_question_id":"488a4731-4103-465c-b5a9-978ddb3b1022:1","marking_criteria":["Explains the importance of the synthesis of new DNA strands at the replication forks","Describes the consequences of a failure in this process"]},{"id":9,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the total number of chromosomes shown in the karyotype — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Reference to the diagram."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the total number of chromosomes shown in the karyotype.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Count each pair as two chromosomes and add the unpaired ones to find the total number of chromosomes in the karyotype.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"The total number of chromosomes is 5 pairs × 2 + 7 pairs × 2 + 6 pairs × 2 + 4 pairs × 2 + 2 unpaired = 10 + 14 + 12 + 8 + 2 = 46 chromosomes.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Correct total number of chromosomes","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":10,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the significance of the two unpaired chromosomes in the inset — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Explanation of their significance; Reference to the diagram."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the significance of the two unpaired chromosomes in the inset.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Unpaired chromosomes can result in genetic disorders due to an abnormal number of chromosomes, which can affect normal development and function.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"The two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small, may indicate a genetic disorder or condition such as aneuploidy, where there is an abnormal number of chromosomes. The presence of unpaired chromosomes can lead to genetic diseases or developmental issues.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Identification of unpaired chromosomes","Explanation of their significance","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":11,"part":"c","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the role of specific genes in normal development and function."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Describe how a mutation in one of the chromosomes might affect an individual.","scenario":"Refer to the diagram showing a karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small.","topic_id":97,"image_alt":"A karyotype of orange chromosomes on a grey background, arranged in pairs in four rows by decreasing size: 5 pairs in the first row, 7 pairs in the second, 6 pairs in the third, and 4 pairs in the fourth. A separate boxed inset at the bottom right holds two unpaired chromosomes, one long and one very small. No labels or numbers are printed.\nLabels: \nOriginal exercise:","image_url":"/textbook-images/rwanda/8/page108-embedded-0.jpeg","explanation":"Mutations can lead to a range of effects depending on the gene involved and the type of mutation, potentially causing genetic disorders or having no noticeable impact.","topic_label":"Chromosomes and Nucleic Acids","model_answer":"A mutation in one of the chromosomes, such as a deletion, duplication, or translocation, can lead to a variety of effects depending on the gene affected. For example, a mutation in a gene responsible for a specific protein might result in a genetic disorder, while a mutation in a non-coding region might have no noticeable effect. The impact of the mutation can range from mild to severe, affecting various aspects of an individual's health and development.","exam_question_id":"452f9bc3-b982-413b-a50d-555ad2ce5bff:1","marking_criteria":["Explanation of mutation effects","Reference to specific chromosomes or genes","Reference to the diagram"]},{"id":12,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Use the genetic code table to find the amino acids."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Name the amino acids that are coded by the mRNA sequence provided.","scenario":"You are given a sample of mRNA with the sequence AUG-UAC-CGA-UGA. Using this sequence, identify the corresponding amino acids produced during translation.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Using the genetic code table, identify the start codon AUG as Methionine (Met). Translate each codon to its respective amino acid. UGA is a stop codon and terminates translation.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"Start codon AUG codes for Methionine (Met). UAC codes for Tyrosine (Tyr). CGA codes for Arginine (Arg). UGA is a stop codon and does not code for an amino acid.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:1","marking_criteria":["Identify the start codon (AUG)","Identify and name the three codons","Match each codon to its corresponding amino acid"]},{"id":13,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain the role of the ribosome during the translation process — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Discuss the role of in this process."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain the role of the ribosome during the translation process.","scenario":"You are given a sample of mRNA with the sequence AUG-UAC-CGA-UGA. Using this sequence, identify the corresponding amino acids produced during translation.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"The ribosome is the site of protein synthesis. It binds to mRNA and ensures the correct sequence of amino acids is produced by reading the mRNA sequence in codons and facilitating the addition of amino acids by tRNA.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The ribosome facilitates protein synthesis by binding to mRNA and facilitating the decoding of the mRNA sequence into a polypeptide chain. It reads the mRNA sequence in codons and ensures the correct amino acids, brought by tRNA molecules, are added to the growing polypeptide chain.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:1","marking_criteria":["Describe the ribosome's role in protein synthesis","Explain how the ribosome reads the mRNA sequence","Discuss the role of tRNA in this process"]},{"id":14,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the temperature at which the rate of protein synthesis is the highest — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: the corresponding temperature."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the temperature at which the rate of protein synthesis is the highest.","scenario":"A scientist is investigating the effect of temperature on the rate of protein synthesis. The following data is collected at different temperatures: \nTemperature (°C): 20, 30, 40, 50 \nRate of Protein Synthesis (proteins/min): 40, 60, 80, 50","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Compare the rates of protein synthesis at each temperature and identify the highest value.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The highest rate of protein synthesis is 80 proteins/min at 40°C.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:3","marking_criteria":["Identify the highest rate of protein synthesis","State the corresponding temperature"]},{"id":15,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Consider the effect of temperature on enzyme structure and function."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of protein synthesis decreases at 50°C despite being higher at 40°C.","scenario":"A scientist is investigating the effect of temperature on the rate of protein synthesis. The following data is collected at different temperatures: \nTemperature (°C): 20, 30, 40, 50 \nRate of Protein Synthesis (proteins/min): 40, 60, 80, 50","topic_id":100,"explanation":"High temperatures can denature enzymes, making them inactive. This reduces the efficiency of the protein synthesis process, resulting in a lower rate of protein synthesis at 50°C.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The rate of protein synthesis decreases at 50°C because high temperatures can denature enzymes, rendering them inactive. This inactivation reduces the efficiency of the translation process, leading to a lower rate of protein synthesis.","exam_question_id":"28b9e1e4-2550-4ea9-a73e-f085de70112a:3","marking_criteria":["Identify the impact of high temperature on enzyme activity","Explain the denaturation of enzymes at high temperatures","Discuss the effect on the rate of protein synthesis"]},{"id":16,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"State the independent variable and the dependent variable in this experiment.","scenario":"During a practical session, students are investigating the effect of different concentrations of a transcription inhibitor on the rate of transcription in yeast cells. They set up the experiment with varying concentrations of the inhibitor and measure the amount of RNA produced over a fixed time period.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"The independent variable is the one you change (concentration of inhibitor), and the dependent variable is what you measure (amount of RNA produced).","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"Independent Variable: Concentration of transcription inhibitor\nDependent Variable: Amount of RNA produced","exam_question_id":"d305d746-7b29-4d07-96a0-5bee2ed76671:2","marking_criteria":["Correctly identify the independent variable.","Correctly identify the dependent variable."]},{"id":17,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Relate the observed to the expected biological outcome."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the amount of RNA produced decreases as the concentration of the inhibitor increases.","scenario":"During a practical session, students are investigating the effect of different concentrations of a transcription inhibitor on the rate of transcription in yeast cells. They set up the experiment with varying concentrations of the inhibitor and measure the amount of RNA produced over a fixed time period.","topic_id":100,"explanation":"Increasing the concentration of the inhibitor reduces the activity of RNA polymerase, which in turn decreases the amount of RNA produced.","subtopic_id":469,"topic_label":"The effect of change in genetic code on the structure of protein during protein synthesis","model_answer":"The transcription inhibitor binds to the RNA polymerase enzyme, reducing its ability to catalyze the transcription process. As the concentration of the inhibitor increases, more enzyme molecules are inhibited, leading to a decrease in the overall rate of transcription. This results in a lower amount of RNA being produced.","exam_question_id":"d305d746-7b29-4d07-96a0-5bee2ed76671:2","marking_criteria":["Explain the role of the transcription inhibitor.","Describe the effect of increasing inhibitor concentration on the transcription process.","Relate the observed results to the expected biological outcome."]},{"id":18,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Look at the table to find the rate of DNA replication that corresponds to the given concentration of DNA polymerase."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Calculate the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 3 units/mL.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication in a laboratory experiment.\n\n| Concentration of DNA Polymerase (units/mL) | Rate of DNA Replication (base pairs/min) |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| 1                                        | 50                                     |\n| 2                                        | 100                                    |\n| 3                                        | 150                                    |\n| 4                                        | 200                                    |\n| 5                                        | 250                                    |","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Using the table, the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 3 units/mL is 150 base pairs/min.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"150 base pairs/min","exam_question_id":"37f21a4c-8b98-4bf9-af7a-cb7502b97f0d:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies the concentration of DNA polymerase from the table.","Correctly identifies the corresponding rate of DNA replication from the table."]},{"id":19,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Correctly identifies that DNA polymerase is an involved in DNA replication.; that a rate of DNA replication is…."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","scenario":"Consider the following table showing the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication in a laboratory experiment.\n\n| Concentration of DNA Polymerase (units/mL) | Rate of DNA Replication (base pairs/min) |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| 1                                        | 50                                     |\n| 2                                        | 100                                    |\n| 3                                        | 150                                    |\n| 4                                        | 200                                    |\n| 5                                        | 250                                    |","topic_id":98,"explanation":"The rate of DNA replication is directly proportional to the concentration of DNA polymerase. DNA polymerase is an enzyme that catalyses the synthesis of DNA. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, more active sites become available for DNA synthesis. This allows for a higher rate of DNA replication because more DNA strands can be synthesised simultaneously. Therefore, an increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase leads to an increase in the rate of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases because DNA polymerase is an enzyme that catalyzes the synthesis of DNA. An increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase provides more active sites for DNA synthesis, allowing for a higher rate of DNA replication. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, more DNA strands can be synthesized simultaneously, leading to a faster overall rate of DNA replication.","exam_question_id":"37f21a4c-8b98-4bf9-af7a-cb7502b97f0d:1","marking_criteria":["Correctly identifies that DNA polymerase is an enzyme involved in DNA replication.","Explains that an increase in the concentration of DNA polymerase leads to more active sites available for DNA synthesis.","Explains that a higher rate of DNA replication is achieved due to the increased number of active sites and faster synthesis of DNA strands."]},{"id":20,"part":"a","type":"longform","hints":["Refer to the data table provided in the scenario."],"marks":2,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Calculate the rate of DNA replication when the concentration of DNA polymerase is 2 units/mL.","scenario":"Consider a laboratory experiment where the concentration of DNA polymerase is varied to observe its effect on the rate of DNA replication. The following data table shows the relationship between the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication.","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Use the data table to find the rate of DNA replication for the given concentration of DNA polymerase.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"The concentration of DNA polymerase is 2 units/mL, which corresponds to a rate of DNA replication of 100 base pairs/min.","exam_question_id":"7a7822e7-cc5a-4c91-a991-d34c059e0bcd:1","marking_criteria":["Identify the correct concentration of DNA polymerase in the table.","Identify the corresponding rate of DNA replication from the table."]},{"id":21,"part":"b","type":"longform","hints":["Stay focused on this: Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases — develop the idea fully rather than listing short points. Make sure you also cover: Provide a logical explanation for the observed trend in the."],"marks":3,"source":{"area":"Effects of improper DNA replication","textbook":"Biology S5 SB"},"checked":true,"question":"Explain why the rate of DNA replication increases as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","scenario":"Consider a laboratory experiment where the concentration of DNA polymerase is varied to observe its effect on the rate of DNA replication. The following data table shows the relationship between the concentration of DNA polymerase and the rate of DNA replication.","topic_id":98,"explanation":"Explain the role of DNA polymerase in DNA replication and how an increase in its concentration can affect the rate of DNA replication.","subtopic_id":459,"topic_label":"Effects of improper DNA replication","model_answer":"DNA polymerase is an enzyme that synthesizes new DNA strands during replication. As the concentration of DNA polymerase increases, there are more enzyme molecules available to catalyze the replication process, leading to a higher rate of DNA replication. This is supported by the data table, which shows an increase in the rate of DNA replication as the concentration of DNA polymerase increases.","exam_question_id":"7a7822e7-cc5a-4c91-a991-d34c059e0bcd:1","marking_criteria":["Explain the role of DNA polymerase in DNA replication.","Explain how an increase in DNA polymerase concentration affects the rate of DNA replication.","Provide a logical explanation for the observed trend in the data table."]},{"id":22,"part"