ing is an example of a correctly written binomial name?","explanation":"'Homo sapiens' is correctly formatted with the genus name capitalized and the species name in lowercase. 'Homo Sapiens' incorrectly capitalizes the species name, 'homo sapiens' incorrectly lowercases the genus name, and 'Homo sapiens sapiens' adds an unnecessary third term.","topic_label":"Mastering The binomial classification system","correct_answer":0},{"id":4,"type":"mcq","source":{"area":"Mastering The binomial classification system"},"options":["To describe the physical characteristics of an organism","To provide a unique name for each species","To indicate the geographical location of the species","To show the evolutionary relationships between species"],"question":"What is the primary purpose of the binomial classification system?","explanation":"The primary purpose is to provide a unique, two-part name for each species, ensuring clarity and consistency in scientific communication.","topic_label":"Mastering The binomial classification system","correct_answer":1},{"id":5,"type":"mcq","source":{"area":"Mastering The binomial classification system"},"options":["Canis lupus","canis lupus","Canis Lupus","Canis lupus lupus"],"question":"Which of the following binomial names is incorrectly formatted?","explanation":"'canis lupus' incorrectly lowercases the genus name, while 'Canis lupus' and 'Canis lupus lupus' are correctly formatted. 'Canis lupus lupus' adds an unnecessary third term but is still correctly formatted.","topic_label":"Mastering The binomial classification system","correct_answer":1},{"id":6,"type":"mcq","source":{"area":"Mastering The binomial classification system"},"options":["The genus name","The species name","The family name","The order name"],"question":"In the binomial classification system, what does the second word represent?","explanation":"The second word in a binomial name represents the species name, which is a specific group within the genus.","topic_label":"Master