s.","Developing a headache.","Getting a sore back from bending."],"question":"A worker is using a chisel and hammer. What is the MOST likely danger associated with this activity?","explanation":"This question assesses the ability to apply knowledge of tool dangers. Chisels and hammers create small, sharp fragments that can become projectiles. These fragments pose a significant risk of cuts and abrasions. Sunburn (A) is unrelated to this tool. A headache (C) is possible but not a direct danger. A sore back (D) is a potential ergonomic issue, but less immediate than flying fragments.","correct_answer":1},{"id":3,"type":"mcq","options":["To make the work area look tidy.","To prevent tripping and falling.","To allow for faster work.","To make it easier to find tools."],"question":"Why is it important to keep the work area clean and free of clutter when using masonry tools?","explanation":"This question tests understanding of safety practices. Clutter creates tripping hazards, increasing the risk of falls and injuries. A clean workspace promotes safe movement. Option A focuses on aesthetics, not safety. Option C is incorrect; clutter slows work. Option D is a minor benefit, not the primary reason for cleanliness.","correct_answer":1},{"id":4,"type":"mcq","options":["Ignore it and continue working.","Apply a bandage and continue working.","Tell a supervisor and get first aid.","Try to clean it yourself with dirty water."],"question":"What should you do FIRST if you accidentally cut yourself while working with a masonry tool?","explanation":"This question assesses knowledge of first aid procedures. Immediately reporting an injury to a supervisor and receiving proper first aid is crucial. Ignoring (A) can lead to infection. Continuing with a bandage (B) might worsen the injury. Dirty water (D) significantly increases infection risk. Proper medical attention is paramount.","correct_answer":2},{"id":5,"type":"mcq","options":["Sandals.","High-heeled shoes.","Steel-toed boots.","Slippers."],"question":"Which of these is an example of appropriate footwear when working on a masonry site?","explanation":"This question tests the application of safety equipment knowledge. Steel-toed boots protect feet from falling objects and sharp materials common on masonry sites. Sandals (A), high heels (B), and slippers (D) offer no protection and are dangerous. Steel-toed boots are specifically designed to prevent foot injuries in construction environments.","correct_answer":2},{"id":6,"type":"mcq","options":["To keep your hands warm.","To improve your grip.","To protect your hands from cuts and abrasions.","To make your hands look clean."],"question":"What is the main purpose of wearing gloves when handling masonry tools and materials?","explanation":"This question assesses understanding of protective gear. Gloves provide a barrier against sharp edges, rough surfaces, and potentially irritating materials, preventing cuts and abrasions. Keeping warm (A) is a secondary benefit. Improved grip (B) is possible, but not the primary reason. Cleanliness (D) is not the purpose of safety gloves. Preventing injury is the main goal.","correct_answer":2},{"id":7,"type":"mcq","options":["Lifting the block quickly.","Bending at the waist to lift the block.","Asking for help or using lifting equipment.","Trying to lift the block with one hand."],"question":"A mason is using a heavy stone block. What is the BEST way to avoid a back injury?","explanation":"This question tests knowledge of safe lifting practices. Lifting heavy objects alone can cause serious back injuries. Seeking assistance or using tools like a pulley or lever reduces strain. Lifting quickly (A) increases risk. Bending at the waist (B) is extremely dangerous. Lifting with one hand (D) is unstable and unsafe. Proper technique or assistance is essential.","correct_answer":2},{"id":8,"type":"mcq","options":["To keep your hands warm in cold weather.","To protect your hands from cuts and burns caused by the cement.","To make your hands look cleaner.","To help you grip the tools better."],"question":"Why is it important to wear gloves when working with cement during masonry?","explanation":"This question tests understanding of health and safety measures in masonry. The correct answer is that gloves protect hands from the corrosive and abrasive effects of cement, which can cause burns and cuts. Option A is incorrect because temperature is not the primary concern. Option C is irrelevant to safety. Option D is also incorrect; gloves do not significantly improve grip and are primarily for protection.","correct_answer":1},{"id":9,"type":"mcq","options":["To improve your vision.","To protect your eyes from flying brick dust and fragments.","To keep the sun out of your eyes.","To make you look like a professional mason."],"question":"What is the MOST important reason for wearing safety goggles when cutting bricks?","explanation":"This question assesses understanding of the dangers of masonry tools and protective measures. Safety goggles are essential to shield the eyes from particles created when cutting bricks. Option A is incorrect as goggles do not improve vision. Option C is irrelevant in most masonry work environments. Option D is a superficial reason and doesn't relate to safety. The primary objective is eye protection.","correct_answer":1},{"id":10,"type":"mcq","options":["Continue working and ignore the cut.","Apply a bandage and keep working.","Immediately clean the wound with water and seek first aid.","Tell their friend about the injury."],"question":"A mason accidentally cuts themselves with a cold chisel. What is the FIRST thing they should do?","explanation":"This question tests applying health and safety procedures in a practical scenario. Immediate cleaning of a wound is crucial to prevent infection. Ignoring the cut (Option A) is dangerous. Applying a bandage without cleaning (Option B) can trap bacteria. Telling a friend (Option D) delays necessary medical att